# The English word "nominal": a jittered delay's pre-jitter value.
docs/reference/specs/run-history.md	17. **Retry policy.** `createRunHistoryWriter({ store, warn, onPersisted?, sleep?, random? })` retries twice with jittered backoff (nominal 1 s then 4 s, each ±50%) on a `TransientStoreError` (network, timeout, 408/429/5xx) or any unclassified error; never on a `PermanentStoreError` (4xx such as 413, malformed response — one warn, `failures() + 1`); a `RouteMissingError` (404 — the deployed Worker predates the run-history routes) logs `[run-history] state Worker has no /runs/put — deploy the state Worker with run-history routes before this bot version` ONCE per process, sets `degraded()`, never retries, and counts every loss. Retries exhausted → one warn naming the run id and attempt count, `failures() + 1`. On success `onPersisted(id)` runs (exception-isolated) — the dispatcher wires it to `registry.markPersisted`, which sets `persisted: true` on the run's `RunSummary` (the key is absent until then) and emits one index `upsert`; a no-op for an unknown or already-evicted run. A 404/413 on `/runs/put` loses that run for the friction ledger too — it reads run history and has no other source.

# The spec lists the class patterns by example.
docs/reference/specs/public-hygiene.md	1. **Five classes, one regex each, tested per line.** `names` (the company, its sibling products, people, the private Slack channel — case-insensitive; a 1Password `op://` reference is an integration's own syntax, not imprint, and a vault path that names the company is caught by the company), `trackers` (`#NNN` issue references and the private org's GitHub URLs; a line anchor, an HTML entity, a heading or a hex colour is not a tracker), `planIds` (`KTD…`, `KD…`, `OQ…`, and the bare `U1`–`U9` / `R1`–`R99` markers; a Slack user id and Cloudflare's `R2` are not), `ids` (Slack channel/user/DM ids and 32-hex account ids), `dates` (a full ISO date). A line can hit several classes; each is counted.

# The spec names "nominal" as the canonical allowed English word.
docs/reference/specs/public-hygiene.md	4. **A legitimate line is allowed by name.** `scripts/public-hygiene.allow` holds `path<TAB>trimmed line` entries with a comment saying why (the English word "nominal", a colour that looks like an issue number, an integration's own identifier). An allowed line is skipped whole. An entry is stale — and fails the check — when its line no longer exists or no longer matches any class, so the file carries neither ghosts nor dead weight.

# The install path names the project's own published artifacts — the npm package (`project.json`'s `npmPackage`, held equal by check:project-facts), the image, the repository URL a contributor clones — the way an operator types them.
CONTRIBUTING.md	published CLI package in `packages/switchboard/` (what `npx @coreplane/switchboard`
README.md	The clone is the install until the CLI is on npm. The tree carries it as the package `@coreplane/switchboard` — the same `src/cli.ts`, bundled with the files it reads — and the release workflow publishes it once the project turns publishing on; from that release, an empty directory is enough:
README.md	`curl -fsSL https://openswitchboard.dev/install.sh | sh` is the same `init` behind a Node version check. The bot itself is the published container image, `ghcr.io/coreplanelabs/switchboard`.
README.md	git clone https://github.com/coreplanelabs/switchboard.git
README.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard ask "what can you do?"
README.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard init --organization <your GitHub org> --anthropic-key <your key>
docs/how-to/deploy.md	- A directory to deploy from. Two shapes, one set of commands: a **checkout** of the repository at `origin/main` with `npm ci` run — what contributors and the release workflow use — or an **operator directory**: any directory, when the CLI is the published npm package (`npx @coreplane/switchboard`, [Get started](../tutorials/get-started.md)). `init --cloudflare` writes the profile there and `deploy plan|init|secrets|config|all` run from it with no clone; what it holds and what still needs a checkout is [below](#deploying-from-the-package). Every `npx tsx src/cli.ts …` on this page is the checkout's CLI; from an operator directory the same command is `npx @coreplane/switchboard …`.
docs/how-to/ship-a-release.md	The same run publishes the bot image to GitHub Container Registry — `ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>:<version>` and `:latest`, with a build-provenance attestation and an SBOM — for the local loop and for anyone running the container outside Cloudflare ([Deploy](deploy.md#running-the-container-somewhere-else) says how to verify it). The deploy does not consume it: wrangler builds the same Dockerfile at deploy time, so the two jobs neither wait on nor fail with each other. A third job can publish the CLI to npm as `@coreplane/switchboard` at the same version, with provenance — what `npx @coreplane/switchboard init` will fetch. It is off until publishing is deliberately turned on, behind two levers so that no release publishes by accident: the package manifest carries `"private": true` (npm refuses to publish it from anywhere; `npm pack` still works, which is what the smoke test uses), and the job runs only when the repository variable `SWITCHBOARD_PUBLISH_NPM` is `true`. Publishing starts with a reviewed pull request that removes `private: true` from `packages/switchboard/package.json` — the visible, historical record of the decision — plus, once, `gh variable set SWITCHBOARD_PUBLISH_NPM --body true` and the `NPM_TOKEN` secret ([Configure the repository](configure-the-repository.md#5-repository-secrets-and-variables)). A release with the switch off carries one `npm publish is off` notice; the image and the deploy publish regardless.
docs/public/install.sh	# and can be run by hand: `npx @coreplane/switchboard init --help`.
docs/public/install.sh	# runs the published installer — `npx @coreplane/switchboard init` — with every
docs/public/install.sh	PACKAGE="@coreplane/switchboard"
docs/reference/specs/README.md	| [packaging.md](packaging.md) | The npm package the release publishes (`@coreplane/switchboard`, the CLI bundled with the files it reads under `dist/assets/`): one resolver finds those files in a checkout, the image and the package; the smoke test installs the tarball and runs it; one release-please component moves the version; `publish-npm` publishes with provenance; the curl front door checks Node and hands off to `npx`; what stays clone-only and why |
docs/reference/specs/packaging.md	1. **One package, the CLI, under the project's scope.** `packages/switchboard/` is a workspace whose name is `project.json`'s `npmPackage` (`@coreplane/switchboard`); `check:project-facts` holds the manifest's `name`, `description`, `license` (the root's), `homepage` (the docs URL), `repository.url` and `.directory`, and `bugs.url` to the facts. `bin` is `switchboard` → `dist/cli.js`; `files` is `dist` alone (npm adds the manifest, README and the LICENSE the build copies in); `engines.node` is `>=<the .nvmrc major>`; `publishConfig` is `access: public, provenance: true`. Its `dependencies` are exactly the npm packages the bundled CLI imports, at the root's ranges — derived by a test from esbuild's metafile, so a dependency the CLI stops or starts importing fails the suite until the manifest follows.
docs/reference/specs/packaging.md	Switchboard is installed, not forked ([one production target](../../decisions/0023-one-production-target.md)). The source stays one public monorepo; what an operator installs are the two artifacts each release publishes: the container image (`ghcr.io/<owner>/<repo>`, [release-and-deploy.md](release-and-deploy.md) item 21) that IS the bot, and the npm package this spec covers — the CLI, so that `npx @coreplane/switchboard init` reaches a running `ask` from an empty directory with no clone. The package is the same CLI the checkout runs (`src/cli.ts`, bundled) and the same files it reads (the examples, the Worker templates, the manifests — copied), under one rule for finding them; nothing is a second implementation.
docs/reference/specs/packaging.md	| The first publish: once publishing is turned on, the next release has a green `publish the npm package` job and the package is on the registry with provenance; until then every release carries the `npm publish is off` notice and the job is skipped (item 5) | `[agent]` **Human-gated once:** a reviewed PR removes `private: true` from the manifest, `NPM_TOKEN` exists as a repository secret and `SWITCHBOARD_PUBLISH_NPM` is `true`. Then, after the next release PR merges: the `release-please` run shows `publish the npm package` green beside `publish the bot image`; `npm view @coreplane/switchboard version` prints the release's version; `npm view @coreplane/switchboard dist.attestations` names a provenance attestation (or the package page shows the "Provenance" panel naming `release-please.yml`); in an empty directory `npx @coreplane/switchboard@latest init --dry-run --organization acme --anthropic-key sk-test` prints `would write:` with `.env` and `config/config.yaml`; `curl -fsSL https://openswitchboard.dev/install.sh \| sh -s -- --dry-run --organization acme --anthropic-key sk-test` prints the same. |
docs/tutorials/get-started.md	**Without the clone, once the CLI is on npm.** The tree carries the same CLI as the package `@coreplane/switchboard` ([packaging](../reference/specs/packaging.md)), and the release workflow publishes it once the project turns publishing on. From that release, Parts 1 and 2 need no checkout: in an empty directory, `npx @coreplane/switchboard init --organization <org> --anthropic-key <your key>` writes the same two files and `npx @coreplane/switchboard ask "what can you do?"` answers the same way; `curl -fsSL https://openswitchboard.dev/install.sh | sh -s -- --organization <org> --anthropic-key <your key>` is the `init` line behind a Node version check (read [`install.sh`](https://openswitchboard.dev/install.sh) first if you would rather not pipe a download into your shell — the `npx` line is the same thing). Until the package is published those commands fail to resolve it, so this lesson is written for the checkout; Part 3 needs the checkout for one step either way — the bot's image is built from the tree — while the rest of the deploy runs from any directory with the package ([Deploy](../how-to/deploy.md#deploying-from-the-package)).
docs/tutorials/get-started.md	git clone https://github.com/coreplanelabs/switchboard.git

# The npm package is the project under its published name — `npmPackage` in project.json, held equal here by check:project-facts — and its manifest and README name the repository and the image the same way the root's do.
packages/switchboard/package.json	"name": "@coreplane/switchboard",
packages/switchboard/package.json	"url": "git+https://github.com/coreplanelabs/switchboard.git",
packages/switchboard/package.json	"url": "https://github.com/coreplanelabs/switchboard/issues"
packages/switchboard/README.md	# @coreplane/switchboard
packages/switchboard/README.md	The OpenSwitchboard command line: the installer and the operator commands of [Switchboard](https://github.com/coreplanelabs/switchboard), an agent gateway — mention it in Slack and an agent reviews the PR, ships the fix, or answers the question, on the model you choose, with its tools running where you decide.
packages/switchboard/README.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard init --organization <your GitHub org> --anthropic-key <your key>
packages/switchboard/README.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard ask "what can you do?"
packages/switchboard/README.md	The bot itself — the always-on process that connects to Slack — is the published container image, `ghcr.io/coreplanelabs/switchboard`. The deploy commands (`init --cloudflare`, `deploy plan|init|secrets|config|all`) run from the directory you ran `init` in, no checkout needed; the bot's own image is the one piece still built from a checkout. Everything else: <https://openswitchboard.dev>.

# The deploy how-to's operator-directory example runs the package by its published name.
docs/how-to/deploy.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard init --organization <org> --anthropic-key <key> --cloudflare <account id> --zone example.com
docs/how-to/deploy.md	npx @coreplane/switchboard deploy plan

# The smoke test's --cloudflare refusal needs an account id of the real shape (32 hex characters); this is the documented synthetic one, not an account.
packages/switchboard/smoke.test.mts	const account = "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef";
